## When the Stack Runs Out

A **stack overflow** occurs when too many stack frames are created — typically from unbounded recursion. The program traps with a stack overflow error.

### What Causes Stack Overflow

```xiom
// Infinite recursion — will overflow the stack
fn infinite(n: Int) -> Int {
  return infinite(n + 1);  // each call adds a stack frame
}

// Deep finite recursion — may overflow for large n
fn deep_recursion(n: Int) -> Int {
  if n == 0 { return 0; }
  return 1 + deep_recursion(n - 1);  // n = 1,000,000 may overflow
}
```

### Preventing Stack Overflow

**Use loops instead of recursion**:

```xiom
// Recursive (risky for large n)
fn sum_recursive(n: Int) -> Int {
  if n == 0 { return 0; }
  return n + sum_recursive(n - 1);
}

// Iterative (safe for any n)
fn sum_iterative(n: Int) -> Int {
  var total = 0;
  var i = 1;
  while i <= n {
    total = total + i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return total;
}
```

### Heap as Alternative

Put large data on the heap instead of the stack:

```xiom
// BAD: large array on stack may overflow
// let huge = [0; 100000];

// GOOD: use Vec (heap-allocated)
let huge = Vec[Int]().with_capacity(100000);
```

### Stack Size

- Default stack size varies by platform (typically 1-8 MB)
- Each function call uses some stack space for locals
- Deep recursion + large local variables = overflow

### Recognizing Overflow

Program terminates with a stack overflow trap — no graceful recovery possible. Prevention is the only cure.

